      "statusChangeID": "6074bf894f898e247f08338f",
      "statusChangeNewStatus": "complete",
      "value": "Now documented in https://docs.vrchat.com/docs/udon-networking#onownershiptransferred\nThe short of it is - use your own prefix that starts with an undersore for now. You can add a unique identifier like using `_u_variableName` which is not a format we will use. We decided that we will add a parameter in the event table in the future to declare events as local/remote capable instead of using this prefix workaround - we plan to increment the Udon Program version for the first time and support this new parameter for all Udon Programs identifying themselves with the new version number. This is a much larger change and will take some time before we can get it in but we feel that it's a better system design overall.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-03-17T00:50:11.642Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "The new event documentation states:\n> If you start your Event names with an underscore, you will not be able to call them over the network. We do this to safeguard our internal methods like _start, _update, _interact against malicious network calls. Best to avoid naming your own methods this way.\nThe recommendation to avoid naming our own events this way contradicts prior advice. However, it remains unclear whether we can safely use some underscore-prefixed event names without clashing with future VRChat-defined internal events. Please clarify what the recommended naming scheme is for user-defined events that should not be network invokable in this documentation.",
